course: dictionary
lesson: 342
title: Annoyance: when things bother us
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Annoyance is the feeling of being a little cross because something bothers you. It is smaller than rage, but it still makes you sigh or frown. «She rolled her eyes with annoyance when the bus was late again.» The words «with annoyance» tell you how she felt, and the rolled eyes show it.
explain: An annoyance can also be a thing or person that bothers you. «The buzzing fly was a constant annoyance during our picnic.» Here the fly is the annoyance itself. Read the words around the word to see which meaning fits.
explain: Some phrases show annoyance too. «You're driving me round the bend!» means you are making me very cross. «I've told you a million times» is not a real count, because the number is made up, but the annoyance behind it is real.
fact: Annoyance means the feeling of being a little angry or cross.
fact: An annoyance is a thing or person that bothers you, such as a dripping tap or a slow computer.
fact: «With annoyance» and «to her annoyance» both show that someone felt cross.
fact: «Round the bend» means mad or very annoyed.
fact: The feeling has no plural, but the thing that bothers you can be made plural: annoyances.
exercise: «The buzzing fly was a constant annoyance during our picnic.» What is the annoyance here? | A happy song | A kind of sandwich | *A thing that keeps bothering us | A sunny day
exercise: «She rolled her eyes with annoyance when the bus was late again.» What does annoyance mean here? | Being surprised and pleased | A loud noise | *A feeling of being a little cross | Being very tired and sleepy
exercise: Ben says «I've told you a million times» means exactly one million times. What is the truth? | It means nothing at all | *The number is made up, but the annoyance is real | It is exactly one million words
test: «You're driving me round the bend!» What does round the bend mean? | Going around a corner | Happy and singing | *Mad, or very annoyed | Lost in a maze
test: What is the plural of «an annoyance», the thing that bothers you? | *annoyances | annoyance | annoyancy | annoyanced
exam: «For Pete's sake, stop tapping!» What does this phrase show? | *Mild annoyance | Deep sadness | Hunger | Great surprise
